{
  "id": "ss.terrain.web",
  "name": "Silk (SS)",
  "description": "The Spider Cave's set silk, drawn from above like everything else on that floor, in the order an orb weaver actually builds one: four frame lines slung across the patch, fifteen radii out of a hub that sits off centre because a real one does, then six courses of sticky spiral, each broken somewhere different so a field of them never repeats. Behind it, five faint threads at another angle \u2014 the corner has been spun before. Caught in it: a wing, a husk, a leg and a scatter of dew, which is what says this floor has already worked. Cold white (`$silent`) on cave stone, because silk is the one thing down there brighter than anything alive. `torn` is the state after somebody has walked through it; `sheet` is the same patch spun by something that does not build orbs \u2014 a mat of short threads with a funnel going down through it \u2014 and `sheet_torn` is that walked through. The game scales all four to whatever radius the patch rolled.",
  "tags": [
    "ss-terrain",
    "web"
  ],
